<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lysimachus</h1><p class="st-meta">306 BCE – 281 BCE · capital Lysimachia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Hellenistic kingdom of Alexander's general Lysimachus, ruling Thrace, Macedon and western Asia Minor before his death at Corupedium.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Alexander's death in 323 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> and spent years subduing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a>. In 309 BCE he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli peninsula</a> as his capital, and around 306 BCE he took the royal title with the other Successors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> was a careful financier who built a large treasury and minted famous tetradrachms showing the deified Alexander. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE he gained most of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, refounding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. North of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Haemus</a> he campaigned against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a>, and around 292 BCE was captured by their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dromichaetes">Dromichaetes</a>. In 288 BCE he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, which he held alone from 285 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Family murders, including the killing of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-son-of-lysimachus">Agathocles</a>, alienated his supporters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> defeated and killed him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium">Corupedium</a> in 281 BCE. His realm broke up, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> soon fell to the Celtic invasions and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tylis">Kingdom of Tylis</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 306 BCE – 281 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:53.61%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>306 BCE – 281 BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lysimachia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lysimachus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bulgaria, Turkey, Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Thrace (Lysimachus), Lysimachid Thrace, Lysimachid kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
